meta:
  origin:
    # Data product / Snapshot
    title: "Accrued-to-date pension entitlements in social insurance"
    description: |-
      Accrued-to-date pension entitlements are the present value of the pensions that will be paid in the future on the basis of rights already earned: the contributions that current workers have already paid and the remaining entitlements of current pensioners. Rights earned after the reference year are not included. The data covers old-age pensions as well as survivor and disability pensions, in all social insurance pension schemes: private schemes, schemes for government employees, and social security pension schemes.

      The data comes from the supplementary table on pensions of the European System of Accounts (ESA 2010), table 29 of its transmission program. Entitlements in private employer schemes and in funded government schemes are recorded in the core national accounts. Entitlements in unfunded government schemes, such as social security, are not, because their participants have no definite claim on them; they are treated as contingent liabilities. Eurostat notes that accrued-to-date pension entitlements are not a measure of the sustainability of public finances and are not part of government debt.

      EU member states and the countries of the European Free Trade Association (EFTA), except Liechtenstein, report the table every three years, two years after the reference year, starting with reference year 2015; some countries report more years voluntarily. The UK reported data for 2015 and 2018, while it was an EU member. Values are given in million euros, in million units of national currency, and as a share of GDP. The estimates rely on actuarial assumptions aligned with those of the Ageing Working Group of the EU’s Economic Policy Committee. For government schemes, the base case uses a discount rate of 3% in real terms (5% in nominal terms) for years up to 2015, and 2% in real terms (4% in nominal terms) afterward; the nominal rate is 4.5% for Poland and Romania and 5% for Hungary, which assume higher inflation. Retirement savings that people take out on their own, independently of their employer and the government, count as life insurance and are not included.
    # NOTE: date_published is the table's last update: "updated" in the Eurostat API metadata
